WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.080
BETH: What are the main characteristics
found in individuals with Autism?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.910
let’s have a look at these
in a little more detail.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.450
JOE: ‘Communication’.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.830
A child with autism may use different ways
to communicate, they may echo words and

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.220
phrases without context, take an adult to
something that they want, rather than ask

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.350
for it and take what is said literally.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.630
Many individuals with autism have
good language skills, whilst others

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.599
will speak very little, or not at
all, though this does not mean they

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:30.010
cannot communicate in other ways.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.980
Individuals with autism typically prefer
communication to be clear and simple.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:36.629
BETH: ‘Social Interaction’.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.680
Social situations are difficult
for individuals with Autism.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:45.110
A child may prefer to play alone and
won’t respond to other adults or children.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.469
Young people and adults typically struggle
with the ‘rules’ of social engagement,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.230
such as when to speak, when to laugh and
when to empathise, and it can inhibit

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:59.110
their understanding of the ‘bigger
picture’ in relationships and contexts.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.470
It may also cause problems in
education and the workplace.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.530
JOE: ‘Repetitive and
Restrictive behaviour’

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.059
Children with autism can display
repetitive behaviour, including

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.770
hand flapping, toe walking,
spinning wheels, lining up toy cars.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.759
Restrictive behaviour may mean a child
may only eat food of a certain colour,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.270
have to walk the same route or only
watch one programme over and over.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:29.389
As they get older individuals with
Autism may have a need for structure

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.870
and will impose their own routines in
order to help make sense of the world and

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:38.640
alleviate anxiety, and these can sometimes
become obsessive behaviours and rituals.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.809
BETH: ‘Sensory sensitivity’

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.060
Sensory sensitivity is common
to the autism spectrum, this

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.090
may be ‘over sensitivity’ or
‘under-sensitivity’ to things such

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.410
as light, sound, touch and heat, or
certain tastes, textures or smells.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.979
Sensory differences are most noticeable
when children are over-sensitive

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.990
to stimuli and may show distress
at loud noises or bright lights.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.960
A young person or adult may also have
an exceptional attention to detail,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:12.140
they may find working in a room
with certain sounds, raised voices,

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.710
flickering lights or cracks in the
wall will adversely affect them.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.930
They may be affected by colours and
textures and the way things are laid

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.440
out, even changes in peoples appearance.

00:02:23.289 --> 00:02:27.750
These sensitivities may lead to various
coping behaviours including avoidance of

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:33.140
certain places or people, rocking or even
using self-injury as a way of calming

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.549
the overload of sensations experienced.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.200
JOE: ‘Fixation’.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.269
Fixation is one of the characteristics
especially associated with high

00:02:42.289 --> 00:02:47.330
functioning Autism spectrum disorder and
may involve subject or ideas fixation,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:52.070
continually discussing the same topics
in conversation, obsessively playing the

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.840
same song repeatedly, or reading every
article written about a certain topic.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.349
Many other high-functioning autistic
individuals have used their focus

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.620
on mathematics, biology, or writing
to inspire successful careers.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.519
However, they may also experience
emotional sensitivity, social

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.959
difficulties, linguistic

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.410
oddities, devotion to routine
and dislike of change.

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:17.450
BETH: Fluctuation of Skills

00:03:18.119 --> 00:03:21.500
Some autistic people have skills
and needs that ‘fluctuate’ from

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.270
day to day and moment to moment.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.990
They may have over the years,
learned strategies which mask their

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.660
difficulties and characteristics.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.009
The result is that the person with
autism can appear to be very distracted

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.039
and may seem to be day-dreaming, as
they are preoccupied with experiences

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:38.769
that others cannot appreciate.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:44.020
Often autistic individuals are seen as
not having the ability to play or role

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.019
play, but that doesn’t mean that they
lack imagination and creative talent.

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:53.190
Some have narrow interests in a particular
topic or pastime and their obsessive

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:57.510
behaviour, means that they may focus
on that, often becoming extremely

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.739
knowledgeable or proficient in it.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.630
This focus on detail can enable high
levels of achievement in certain fields.

